      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we first need to determine an appropriate equivalent angle within the standard range of \\(0\\) to \\(2\\pi\\) or \\(-\\pi\\) to \\(\\pi\\). This is because trigonometric functions are periodic with a period of \\(2\\pi\\), meaning \\(\\sin x = \\sin(x + 2\\pi k)\\) for any integer \\(k\\).\n\nStarting with \\(\\frac{11\\pi}{3}\\), we first convert this to a more manageable form. We can do this by subtracting \\(2\\pi\\) (which is \\(\\frac{6\\pi}{3}\\)) until the angle is between \\(0\\) and \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{11\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = \\frac{11\\pi}{3} - \\frac{6\\pi}{3} = \\frac{5\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is in the second quadrant (between \\(\\pi\\) and \\(2\\pi\\)) because \\(180^\\circ < \\frac{5\\pi}{3} < 360^\\circ\\). In the second quadrant, the sine function is positive.\n\nNext, we find the reference angle for \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\). The reference angle is the smallest angle that the terminal side of the given angle makes with the x-axis. By subtracting \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) from \\(2\\pi\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n2\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{6\\pi}{3} - \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nSo, the sine of \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) is the same as the sine of the reference angle \\(\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{3} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\). Since we identified \\(\\frac{5\\pi}{3}\\) as in the second quadrant where the sine function is positive, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}\n\\]",
