      "text": "$\\sin \\frac{11 \\pi}{3} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$.\n\n***\n\nEvaluate $\\sin \\left(\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}\\right).$ Record your answer in simplest radical form.\n\n\nSolution 1:\n$\\frac{\\textrm{Area of sector}}{\\sqrt{1 - \\left(\\frac{\\textrm{Area of sector}}{1}\\right)^2}} = \\sin \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\textrm{Area of sector}}{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\frac{2 \\pi}{4} = \\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$.\n\nSolution 2:\n$\\frac{\\textrm{Angle of sector}}{\\pi} = \\sin \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$\n\nTherefore,  $\\frac{1}{4}(\\pi - 2\\pi) = \\boxed{\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}}$ \n\nSolve the following mathematics problem. Show your complete solution. 25% of the vets in a certain hospital are able to examine a cat, 30% are able to examine a dog, and 10% are able to examine both cats and dogs but not birds. 15% of the vets cannot examine any living animal. By how many percent is the average number of living animals examined per vet less than the smaller of the two percentages? (Round to the nearest percent.)\n\nTo solve this problem, let's start by defining the variables and understanding the given information. We will then use algebraic methods to find the solution.\n\n1. **Define the variables:**\n   - Let \\( S \\) be the total number of vets in the hospital.\n   - Let \\( C \\) be the fraction of vets who can examine cats.\n   - Let \\( D \\) be the fraction of vets who can examine dogs.\n   - Let \\( B \\) be the fraction of vets who can examine both cats and dogs.\n   - Let \\( R \\) be the fraction of vets who cannot examine any living animal.\n\n2. **Given data:**\n   - \\( C = 0.25 \\)\n   - \\( D = 0.30 \\)\n   - \\( B = 0.10 \\)\n   - \\( R = 0.15 \\)\n\n3. **Calculate the overall fraction of vets who can examine animals:**\n   Since 15% of the vets cannot examine any animal, the remaining will be able to examine at least one animal. These vets form the fraction that is \\( 1 - R \\):\n   \\[\n   1 - R = 1 - 0.15 = 0.85\n   \\]\n   Therefore, \\( 0.85 \\) of the vets can examine at least one animal.\n\n4. **Express the fraction of vets that can examine all animals:**\n   The vets that can examine both cats and dogs are those who can examine both animals but not birds, which is 10% of the vets. These vets form the fraction \\( B \\):\n   \\[\n   0.10\n   \\]\n\n5. **Form the equation for the total fraction of vets that can examine animals:**\n   Let \\( x \\) be the fraction of vets that can examine both cats and dogs. The equation combining the fractions of vets that can examine any animal is:\n   \\[\n   C \\cdot (1 - x) + B \\cdot x + C \\cdot x = 0.85\n   \\]\n   Substituting \\( C \\) and \\( B \\):\n   \\[\n   0.25 (1 - x) + 0.10 x + 0.25 x = 0.85\n   \\]\n   Simplify the equation:\n   \\[\n   0.25 - 0.25x + 0.30x + 0.25x = 0.85\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   0.25 + 0.30x = 0.85\n   \\]\n   Solve for \\( x \\):\n   \\[\n   0.30x = 0.60\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{0.60}{0.30} = 2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate the average number of animals examined per vet:**\n   The average fraction of animals examined per vet is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Average fraction} = \\frac{C + D - B}{2} = \\frac{0.25 + 0.30 - 0.10}{2} = \\frac{0.45}{2} = 0.225\n   \\]\n\n7. **Determine the smaller percentage:**\n   The smaller percentage is:\n   \\[\n   0.20 \\text{ (cats)}, \\quad 0.30 \\text{ (dogs)}\n   \\]\n   The smaller percentage is \\( 25\\% \\).\n\n8. **Calculate the difference between the smaller percentage and the average:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Difference} = 25\\% - 22.5\\% = 2.5\\%\n   \\]\n\n9. **Round the result to the nearest percent:**\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{3}\n   \\]",
